he conversion more carefully to see if it's actually March 24th instead.\n\nRather than getting bogged down in timestamp verification, I'll focus on the actual work: compiling a progress summary and documenting it in the shared pool. The cron job timestamp itself shows April 14th at 7:11 PM UTC, which aligns with the system context. For the \"dead\" designation, I'm checking if tasks exceed 60 minutes without updates—but with timestamps spanning months, nearly everything qualifies as dead. The cron job itself is marked as running right now at 7:11 PM, so there's a timing issue to clarify there. I'm calculating the age of these tasks based on the current timestamp. The task监督 is recent at about 2 days old, but the customer analysis task is extremely stale—around 40 days old, which suggests it was created in March and likely completed or abandoned. The other tasks like daily reports and sales outreach plans are also quite old, ranging from about 4 to 40 days, indicating they're either stuck in progress or were never updated.\n\nMost of these tasks show timestamps in March or April, with a few from May, which means they're all well beyond the 60-minute threshold and should be marked as dead. Since I'm running this as an isolated cron job without access to previous context, I'll report that there are stale tasks and proceed with updating the heartbeat and logging the progress to the shared knowledge base, noting that tasks older than 60 minutes should be flagged. The task监督 item is only about 4 days old, so that's not stale, but most of the others haven't been updated in over a month and definitely need to be marked as Dead. I'll record the progress and then add a note about these stale tasks."},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me update heartbeat and write the progress report to the shared pool:\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_o79o3jd32gez_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cat > ~/workspace/agent/workspace/memory/heartbeat-state.json << 'EOF'\n{\n  \"la